WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.179
While she was at Aunt March’s, Amy felt as though she were trapped in a pitch-black tunnel with no light beckoning to her at the end.

2
00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:15.084
Until then, Amy had taken for granted the liveliness and warm affection of her family.

3
00:00:15.085 --> 00:00:21.956
How she missed the constant cheerful voices that seemed to fill the whole house all day long.

4
00:00:21.957 --> 00:00:28.781
"Did I ask you to stop?" Aunt March snapped when Amy, who was reading to her, paused to think about her family.

5
00:00:28.782 --> 00:00:33.580
"Stop!" screeched Polly as she took the cracker Aunt March offered her.

6
00:00:33.581 --> 00:00:39.752
"Forgive me, Aunt March. I’ll continue . . . ," said Amy, returning her attention to the story.

7
00:00:39.753 --> 00:00:44.224
Aunt March might have been a nasty woman but she was not heartless.

8
00:00:44.225 --> 00:00:50.557
She was also worried about the fate of Beth, who she thought was witless but pure-hearted and innocent.

9
00:00:50.558 --> 00:00:55.726
"What have you heard about your sister?" This time she interrupted Amy’s reading.

10
00:00:55.727 --> 00:00:59.345
Fiddling with a corner of the page she was reading, Amy replied,

11
00:00:59.346 --> 00:01:06.661
"Laurie says that Beth is coping better but her fever still has to drop, and she remains stable but weak."

12
00:01:06.662 --> 00:01:11.658
"And what of your Father? Will he be coming home soon?" asked Aunt March.

13
00:01:11.659 --> 00:01:20.862
Shaking her head, Amy said tearfully, "According to the last message we got, Father doesn’t have the strength yet to make the journey home."

14
00:01:20.863 --> 00:01:27.241
"Rubbish! Your sister needs your mother more than he does," Aunt March remarked in a gruff voice.

15
00:01:27.242 --> 00:01:34.281
"Hannah says it’s best not to tell Mother just yet, since there isn’t much she can do here anyway," said Amy.

16
00:01:34.282 --> 00:01:44.292
Aunt March felt a pang in her heart as she watched Amy struggle to control her emotions. "I have a gift for you," she said in a kinder voice.

17
00:01:44.293 --> 00:01:51.286
Amy, wiping her tears (with a silk handkerchief, like a true lady), turned to her with curiosity.

18
00:01:51.287 --> 00:01:56.990
Aunt March held out a small green velvety box, which Amy slowly took from her.

19
00:01:56.991 --> 00:02:05.400
"Oh, Aunt March! I can’t accept this," gasped Amy, holding up an emerald ring that flashed brilliantly in the light.

20
00:02:05.401 --> 00:02:15.430
"Yes, you can and you will, because I am giving it to you," Aunt March replied in a firm and strict voice that meant Amy should not try to argue with her.

21
00:02:15.431 --> 00:02:22.484
"It’s beautiful," Amy said, her eyes dancing joyously as she put the ring on her middle finger.

22
00:02:22.485 --> 00:02:29.109
"I’m glad you like it. Now get back to the story," snapped Aunt March as she fed Polly another cracker.

23
00:02:29.110 --> 00:02:38.028
In addition to the ring, Aunt March promised Amy many more gifts as long as she remained obedient and found favor in the old lady’s eyes.

24
00:02:38.029 --> 00:02:45.373
Upon his next daily visit, Amy promptly told this all to Laurie, who looked doubtful as he listened patiently.

25
00:02:45.374 --> 00:02:52.438
"Aunt March says emeralds are the most precious gems in the world," Amy said, sticking her hand out for him to admire.

26
00:02:52.439 --> 00:02:58.945
"It’s very lovely, but don’t you think you’re a little too young for that?" asked Laurie with a frown.

27
00:02:58.946 --> 00:03:03.880
"Age means nothing if you are a true lady," Amy said knowingly.

28
00:03:03.881 --> 00:03:09.212
"Aunt March says I have the potential to become a respected woman of society."

29
00:03:09.213 --> 00:03:16.870
Laurie mumbled his opinion of Aunt March under his breath, but apparently Amy still had much more to say about material things.

30
00:03:16.871 --> 00:03:23.041
"Aunt March says I’m to have her pearl necklace, earrings, and diamond brooch after she dies!"

31
00:03:23.042 --> 00:03:27.061
"So you want her to die, do you?" Laurie asked nastily.

32
00:03:27.062 --> 00:03:31.819
Reddening in the face, Amy stuttered, "No. What I meant was . . ."

33
00:03:31.820 --> 00:03:36.421
"Do you want to know how Beth is or not?" Laurie asked rather harshly.

34
00:03:36.422 --> 00:03:46.907
Caught up in her own fantasy world, Amy had forgotten at that moment why Laurie was visiting her every day. "Of course, Laurie," she said,

35
00:03:46.908 --> 00:03:51.382
shamefaced. "Well, Beth is still . . . ," Laurie began more

36
00:03:51.383 --> 00:03:54.519
gently. As he spoke, Amy looked down at the

37
00:03:54.520 --> 00:04:00.735
ring. It didn’t seem to shine as beautifully anymore, nor did the thought of a pearl necklace seem so

38
00:04:00.736 --> 00:04:06.830
appealing. The reality was that Beth was gravely sick, and death was no joking

39
00:04:06.831 --> 00:04:10.886
matter. After Laurie had left, Amy had a lot to think

40
00:04:10.887 --> 00:04:14.943
about. She stared at herself in the mirror for a very long

41
00:04:14.944 --> 00:04:20.641
time. Speaking to her reflection, Amy said, "One day you’re going to die,

42
00:04:20.642 --> 00:04:24.986
too." "Can I take these things with me to my grave?" she

43
00:04:24.987 --> 00:04:31.998
wondered. Struck by the truth, she was forced to realize that her possessions would have no value to her once she were

44
00:04:31.999 --> 00:04:41.240
dead. After much soul searching, Amy decided to write a will, reasoning, "I want the people I love to have my things so they’ll remember me by

45
00:04:41.241 --> 00:04:46.410
them." Armed with pen and paper, Amy began to write her last will and

46
00:04:46.411 --> 00:04:54.735
testament: I, Amy Curtis March, being of sound mind, do give all my possessions to the following persons

47
00:04:54.736 --> 00:05:03.054
named: To Father, all my drawings and sketches—those I kept since I was a little girl up to the ones he never

48
00:05:03.055 --> 00:05:08.454
saw. To Mother, all my clothes, except for the blue dress with pink and white

49
00:05:08.455 --> 00:05:11.818
flowers. That one I want Kitty to have. See

50
00:05:11.819 --> 00:05:21.911
below. To Meg, I give the emerald ring from Aunt March, because it is the perfect match for the color of your eyes. The velvet box I also

51
00:05:21.912 --> 00:05:30.785
give. To Jo, I give you all my hair ribbons so that when your hair grows long again, you will have beautiful, colorful ribbons to decorate it

52
00:05:30.786 --> 00:05:34.917
with. I also give you all my pens for you to write more stories

53
00:05:34.918 --> 00:05:43.105
with. To Beth (if she survives), all my dolls, my little play set of teacups, and pink

54
00:05:43.106 --> 00:05:51.247
slippers. To Laurie, I leave you my handkerchiefs with my initials embroidered on them so you will always remember me as a true

55
00:05:51.248 --> 00:05:55.529
lady. To Mr. Laurence, I give you my sculptures of your

56
00:05:55.530 --> 00:06:00.661
horses. I especially loved Harris, and there are two paintings of him that I hope you

57
00:06:00.662 --> 00:06:07.911
like. To Kitty Bryant, my blue dress with pink flowers and my bead necklace, which she always

58
00:06:07.912 --> 00:06:13.078
admired. To Hannah, my collection of seashells and the purple box I kept them

59
00:06:13.079 --> 00:06:17.736
in. And now, after leaving my possessions in the care of those I love and

60
00:06:17.737 --> 00:06:26.859
trust, I want to say how much I love you all and that each one of you has a special place in my heart. I trust we shall meet

61
00:06:26.860 --> 00:06:31.129
again. Signed: Amy Curtis

62
00:06:31.130 --> 00:06:36.411
March When Amy finished writing her will, a peaceful calm came over

63
00:06:36.412 --> 00:06:44.663
her.She prayed, "God, if you allow Beth to live, I promise to be a better sister, daughter, and friend.

64
00:06:44.664 --> 00:06:49.175
Please give me the opportunity to prove that I have really changed."

65
00:06:49.176 --> 00:06:55.182
The next day when Laurie came, Amy showed him the will and asked him to sign it as a witness.

66
00:06:55.183 --> 00:07:00.265
Amused yet touched by Amy’s maturity and thoughtfulness, Laurie agreed.

67
00:07:00.266 --> 00:07:06.205
Then he gave Amy the good news. "Mother March is coming home soon," he announced.

68
00:07:06.206 --> 00:07:11.192
"Beth? Does that mean . . ." Amy couldn’t even say the words.

69
00:07:11.193 --> 00:07:18.103
Laurie took a deep breath and said, "I don’t know, but Dr. Bangs said to send for your mother.

70
00:07:18.104 --> 00:07:21.901
The girls don’t know yet. I want it to be a surprise."

71
00:07:21.902 --> 00:07:25.299
Amy was overjoyed that Mother was returning.

72
00:07:25.300 --> 00:07:31.589
She wondered whether her homecoming would be a happy or sad one.

